course: dictionary
lesson: 1922
title: Clues in brackets and dashes
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words are old, so you will not hear them in everyday talk. When you meet one, look at the words that come right after it, because writers often explain a hard word there. A comfit is an old word for a small sweet made from a seed or nut, such as aniseed, caraway or an almond, coated in hard sugar.
explain: Look at this: «Alice pulls out a box of comfits (sugar-coated sweets) that the salt water had not got into.» The brackets tell you what comfits are: sugar-coated sweets. Whenever you see brackets, a dash or the words "that is", ask yourself whether the writer is explaining the word before them.
explain: Now test your meaning. Try «little sugar sweets» in the sentence: «she has a box of comfits (little sugar sweets) in her pocket, exactly one each.» It still makes sense, so you have worked out the word. A good check is to swap your meaning in and read the sentence again.
fact: A comfit is a noun: a comfit, comfits.
fact: «Comfit» means a small sweet made from a seed or nut, coated in hard sugar.
fact: Comfits were eaten in Tudor and Victorian times, a bit like sugared almonds or sprinkles today.
fact: In «Alice in Wonderland», Alice hands round a box of comfits, one each, after the Caucus-race.
exercise: What is a comfit? | A kind of race | *A small sweet coated in hard sugar | A thimble | A large bird
exercise: The text says «a-piece» means each. In «there is exactly one a-piece, all round», what does a-piece mean? | *Each | Everybody has won | Hidden in a pocket | All the sweets are gone
exercise: Which of these is NOT a seed or nut used to make a comfit? | Aniseed | Caraway | Almond | *Gravel
test: In «Alice in Wonderland», what does Alice hand round after the Caucus-race? | A teapot | A pair of shoes | *A box of comfits | A map of the country
test: When were comfits eaten? | *In Tudor and Victorian times | Only by birds | Only in the Stone Age | Only in the year 2000
exam: Which sentence best explains the word comfit? | A ribbon given as a prize | *A small sweet made from a seed or nut, coated in hard sugar | A sharp stone used to build castles | A type of bird that lives near water
